{
  "configuration_id": "config_003",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_002 (rna_only, multiprofile, pca_n2000, avgpool) achieved the best validation accuracy (0.591) and F1 (0.573), substantially outperforming singleprofile RNA (0.485) and image-only resnet50 (0.208). This establishes that multiprofile RNA with a simple aggregation is the current strongest signal. Uncertainty: We have not tested whether the aggregation method matters—i.e., whether a transformer can capture richer inter-profile relationships beyond simple averaging. Question tested: config_003 is identical to config_002 except it replaces avgpool with a transformer aggregator, isolating the effect of aggregation architecture. Why this is the best next experiment: (1) It is a minimal, controlled extension of the best result, making the comparison clean and interpretable. (2) The aggregation choice is a design axis that propagates into every multimodal configuration (configs 018–051), so resolving it now reduces uncertainty across the largest portion of the remaining search space. (3) If transformer significantly outperforms avgpool, it signals that profile-to-profile interactions carry predictive signal worth modeling; if they are comparable, we confirm that simple pooling suffices and can focus budget on RNA encoder choice and multimodal fusion. Under a limited budget, this single experiment maximizes information per trial by resolving a high-leverage design decision before committing to more complex multimodal configurations."
}

